    "result_text": "The test infrastructure supports audit-log assertions but the current test doesn't assert the format, and my change is additive. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), assigned at creation and persisted, so it round-trips through GET and is recorded in the create audit line.\n\n**Computation** (`AssignOwnerMembershipLevel.java`, new) \u2014 a single small function with one responsibility: start at 1, +1 for an email, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 (with a `MAX_LEVEL` constant noting level 4 is reserved for tenure). Wired into `owners.POST.yml` right after `assignNamesakeCount` (so the count it reads is final) and under the same transaction, mirroring the existing `Assign*` steps.\n\n**Storage** \u2014 added a `membershipLevel` field/column to `Owner` and to all four DB schemas (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres), alongside the other creation-derived fields.\n\n**Response** \u2014 in `openapi.yml`, swapped the `membershipTier` string property for an integer `membershipLevel`; the DTO regenerates from this. In `OwnerMapper` I removed the `membershipTier` `@Mapping` and its now-dead derivation method \u2014 `membershipLevel` auto-maps by name from the stored field, so no mapper logic duplicates the level calculation. This also drops the old `GOLD`/householdSize branch, which the new spec doesn't use.\n\n**Audit** \u2014 `AuditOwnerCreated` now records `membershipLevel=\u2026` on the create line, reading the value already assigned on the entity (no recomputation).\n\nThe acceptance test (`membershipLevel` = 3 for a unique owner with an email) passes, and the audit output confirms `membershipLevel=3`.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec said \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level\" \u2014 the audit line didn't previously record the tier, so I interpreted this as an instruction to have it record the level, and added it there.",
